1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,326
It's 33 °C out, and Jordan
has been running for an hour.

2
00:00:03,376 --> 00:00:06,958
Her muscles are pumping out heat
the whole time, and yet her core

3
00:00:07,008 --> 00:00:09,614
temperature has only
crept up a degree or two.

4
00:00:09,664 --> 00:00:15,150
What's keeping it there is the
sweat on her skin, plain water.

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:16,168
So here's the question.

6
00:00:16,218 --> 00:00:21,635
How can a little water drying off your
skin pull that much heat out of your

7
00:00:21,685 --> 00:00:22,485
body?

8
00:00:22,625 --> 00:00:25,448
By the end of this video,
you'll know exactly why.

9
00:00:25,498 --> 00:00:29,188
And you'll see the same one idea
explain how a water strider stands on

10
00:00:29,238 --> 00:00:34,916
a pond, how water climbs to the top
of a tree, why ice floats, and

11
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:38,348
why salt dissolves but oil doesn't.

12
00:00:38,398 --> 00:00:42,868
It's topic 1.1 of AP Biology, and
it sets up almost everything after

13
00:00:42,918 --> 00:00:43,678
it.

14
00:00:43,698 --> 00:00:46,801
Most students learn the properties
of water as a list to memorize.

15
00:00:46,851 --> 00:00:51,415
Cohesion, adhesion, high
specific heat, and so on.

16
00:00:51,465 --> 00:00:53,828
But they all come from one thing.

17
00:00:53,878 --> 00:00:58,818
Water molecules are polar, so they
stick to each other, and to other

18
00:00:58,868 --> 00:01:03,588
charged things. If you understand why
they stick, you never have to memorize

19
00:01:03,638 --> 00:01:07,158
the list again. So let's
start with one molecule.

20
00:01:10,208 --> 00:01:11,891
Everything is made of atoms.

21
00:01:11,941 --> 00:01:16,818
An atom has a tiny center, the nucleus,
made of protons, which are positive,

22
00:01:16,868 --> 00:01:19,187
and neutrons, which have no charge.

23
00:01:19,237 --> 00:01:23,785
Around it is a cloud of much
lighter electrons, which are negative.

24
00:01:23,835 --> 00:01:26,254
Atoms join by sharing electrons.

25
00:01:26,304 --> 00:01:30,838
When two atoms share a pair of
electrons, that's a covalent bond, and

26
00:01:30,888 --> 00:01:34,648
a group of atoms held together
by covalent bonds is a molecule.

27
00:01:35,148 --> 00:01:40,378
A water molecule is one oxygen
atom bonded to two hydrogen atoms.

28
00:01:40,428 --> 00:01:46,508
That's H2O. Two covalent bonds,
each one a shared pair of electrons.

29
00:01:47,008 --> 00:01:47,851
But here's the thing.

30
00:01:47,901 --> 00:01:49,638
The sharing isn't fair.

31
00:01:49,688 --> 00:01:54,918
Electronegativity is how strongly an
atom pulls on the electrons it shares.

32
00:01:54,968 --> 00:02:01,055
Oxygen pulls hard, because its
nucleus has 8 protons, and the shared

33
00:02:01,105 --> 00:02:02,931
pair sits close to it.

34
00:02:02,981 --> 00:02:08,065
Hydrogen's nucleus has just
one proton, so it pulls weakly.

35
00:02:08,115 --> 00:02:11,702
So the shared electrons spend
more of their time near the oxygen.

36
00:02:11,752 --> 00:02:16,790
That's called a polar covalent bond,
polar meaning it has two ends with

37
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:17,888
different charges.

38
00:02:18,388 --> 00:02:23,076
Because the electrons crowd toward the
oxygen, the oxygen end gets a small

39
00:02:23,126 --> 00:02:27,743
negative charge. We write that with
the Greek letter delta and a minus

40
00:02:27,793 --> 00:02:32,111
sign, delta minus, meaning
a partial negative charge.

41
00:02:32,161 --> 00:02:36,852
Each hydrogen, with its electrons
pulled away, gets a partial positive

42
00:02:36,902 --> 00:02:42,183
charge, delta plus. These aren't
full charges, like an ion, an atom that

43
00:02:42,233 --> 00:02:44,255
has gained or lost a whole electron.

44
00:02:44,305 --> 00:02:45,433
Just a lean.

45
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:48,683
Now the shape matters too.

46
00:02:48,733 --> 00:02:50,383
Water isn't a straight line.

47
00:02:50,433 --> 00:02:55,327
It's bent, at an angle of about 104.5°,
because oxygen also has two pairs

48
00:02:55,377 --> 00:02:59,492
of electrons it doesn't share, and they
push the two bonds closer together.

49
00:02:59,542 --> 00:03:04,743
So both hydrogens sit on the same
side, and the oxygen sits on the

50
00:03:04,793 --> 00:03:09,711
other. That gives the whole molecule
a negative end and a positive end.

51
00:03:09,761 --> 00:03:11,674
Water is a polar molecule.

52
00:03:12,174 --> 00:03:13,556
And the bend really is essential.

53
00:03:13,606 --> 00:03:16,532
Carbon dioxide also has polar bonds.

54
00:03:16,582 --> 00:03:22,072
Each oxygen shares two pairs of
electrons with carbon, a double bond, and

55
00:03:22,122 --> 00:03:23,321
pulls them away from the carbon.

56
00:03:23,371 --> 00:03:28,745
But carbon dioxide is a straight
line, with one oxygen on each side,

57
00:03:28,795 --> 00:03:32,555
so the two pulls point in
opposite directions and cancel out.

58
00:03:32,605 --> 00:03:36,985
Carbon dioxide has no
negative end and no positive end.

59
00:03:37,035 --> 00:03:42,359
It's nonpolar. Polar bonds, plus
a bent shape, that's what makes water

60
00:03:42,409 --> 00:03:43,209
polar.

61
00:03:43,362 --> 00:03:48,023
Quick check. Why does the oxygen
end of a water molecule carry a partial

62
00:03:48,073 --> 00:03:49,109
negative charge?

63
00:03:53,609 --> 00:03:57,609
Because oxygen is more
electronegative than hydrogen.

64
00:03:57,659 --> 00:04:01,689
It pulls the shared electrons
closer, so they spend more time on its

65
00:04:01,739 --> 00:04:05,299
side. Now let's put a second
water molecule next to this one.

66
00:04:08,349 --> 00:04:10,955
What happens when two
water molecules meet?

67
00:04:11,005 --> 00:04:13,899
Opposite charges attract.

68
00:04:13,949 --> 00:04:18,989
The partial positive hydrogen of one
molecule is pulled toward the partial

69
00:04:19,039 --> 00:04:21,259
negative oxygen of its neighbor.

70
00:04:21,309 --> 00:04:24,299
That attraction is a hydrogen bond.

71
00:04:24,349 --> 00:04:28,539
We draw it as a dashed line, to
show it's much weaker than the solid

72
00:04:28,589 --> 00:04:30,709
covalent bonds inside each molecule.

73
00:04:31,209 --> 00:04:36,055
Each water molecule has two hydrogens
it can offer, and two unshared

74
00:04:36,105 --> 00:04:39,228
pairs on its oxygen that
can each attract a hydrogen.

75
00:04:39,278 --> 00:04:43,671
So one water molecule can hydrogen
bond with up to four neighbors at

76
00:04:43,721 --> 00:04:48,242
once. And every neighbor is linked
to more neighbors, so the whole glass

77
00:04:48,292 --> 00:04:51,185
of water is one huge, shifting network.

78
00:04:51,685 --> 00:04:56,328
A single hydrogen bond is weak, roughly
one twentieth as strong as a covalent

79
00:04:56,378 --> 00:05:01,742
bond. And in liquid water, each one
lasts only a tiny fraction of a second.

80
00:05:01,792 --> 00:05:04,855
It breaks, and a new one
forms somewhere else.

81
00:05:04,905 --> 00:05:09,368
But there are so many of them, all
the time, that together they give

82
00:05:09,418 --> 00:05:11,965
water almost every property in this video.

83
00:05:12,465 --> 00:05:14,335
Here's a trap that shows up on tests.

84
00:05:14,385 --> 00:05:21,135
A covalent bond is inside one
molecule, oxygen to its own hydrogens.

85
00:05:21,185 --> 00:05:26,625
A hydrogen bond is between molecules,
the hydrogen of one to the oxygen

86
00:05:26,675 --> 00:05:32,535
of another. So when water boils,
the hydrogen bonds between molecules

87
00:05:32,585 --> 00:05:35,668
break, but each molecule stays whole.

88
00:05:35,718 --> 00:05:37,065
Steam is still H2O.

89
00:05:37,565 --> 00:05:41,219
And hydrogen bonds aren't
only a water thing.

90
00:05:41,269 --> 00:05:46,062
They form wherever a hydrogen attached
to oxygen or nitrogen meets another

91
00:05:46,112 --> 00:05:50,845
oxygen or nitrogen. You'll see them
again holding DNA and proteins together.

92
00:05:51,345 --> 00:05:56,237
Quick check. When an ice cube
melts, are covalent bonds breaking, or

93
00:05:56,287 --> 00:05:57,385
hydrogen bonds?

94
00:06:01,885 --> 00:06:07,475
Hydrogen bonds. Melting only
loosens the links between molecules.

95
00:06:07,525 --> 00:06:11,315
The covalent bonds inside
each H2O are untouched.

96
00:06:11,365 --> 00:06:14,365
Now let's see what all
that sticking actually does.

97
00:06:17,415 --> 00:06:21,205
Cohesion is water sticking to
water, through hydrogen bonds.

98
00:06:21,255 --> 00:06:23,745
Look at the surface of this pond.

99
00:06:23,795 --> 00:06:27,485
A molecule in the middle gets
pulled by neighbors in every direction.

100
00:06:27,535 --> 00:06:32,285
But a molecule at the top has no
water above it, only air, so its

101
00:06:32,335 --> 00:06:34,565
neighbors pull it sideways and down.

102
00:06:34,615 --> 00:06:38,421
The surface gets pulled
tight, like a stretched skin.

103
00:06:38,471 --> 00:06:40,095
That's surface tension.

104
00:06:40,595 --> 00:06:42,288
And it's strong enough
to hold up an insect.

105
00:06:42,338 --> 00:06:47,725
This water strider weighs so little
that it just dents the surface, and

106
00:06:47,775 --> 00:06:49,395
the hydrogen bonds hold it up.

107
00:06:49,895 --> 00:06:56,586
Adhesion is water sticking to a different
surface, one that's polar or charged,

108
00:06:56,636 --> 00:07:00,658
like glass, paper, or the
walls of tubes inside a plant.

109
00:07:00,708 --> 00:07:05,588
Water's partial charges are attracted
to the charges on that surface.

110
00:07:06,088 --> 00:07:09,565
Put the two together in a narrow
tube and something cool happens.

111
00:07:09,615 --> 00:07:16,178
Adhesion pulls water up along the
walls, and cohesion drags the water

112
00:07:16,228 --> 00:07:17,725
in the middle along with it.

113
00:07:17,775 --> 00:07:23,965
The water climbs. That's capillary
action, and it's why a paper towel

114
00:07:24,015 --> 00:07:25,175
soaks up a spill.

115
00:07:25,675 --> 00:07:31,165
Now, the big one. How does water get
from the roots of a tree all the way

116
00:07:31,215 --> 00:07:33,721
up to leaves that might be 50 meters high?

117
00:07:33,771 --> 00:07:40,249
Plants carry water in xylem,
bundles of narrow, hollow tubes made of

118
00:07:40,299 --> 00:07:45,922
dead cells. And the leaves have
tiny pores called stomata, each one

119
00:07:45,972 --> 00:07:48,915
opened and closed by
a pair of guard cells.

120
00:07:49,415 --> 00:07:54,346
When the stomata are open, water
evaporates inside the leaf and escapes

121
00:07:54,396 --> 00:08:00,213
as vapor. That loss of water vapor
from a plant is called transpiration.

122
00:08:00,263 --> 00:08:01,173
And here's the key.

123
00:08:01,223 --> 00:08:07,720
Every molecule that leaves pulls on the
molecule behind it, because cohesion

124
00:08:07,770 --> 00:08:10,965
links them in one continuous chain.

125
00:08:11,015 --> 00:08:15,193
That pull passes all the way down
the water column in the xylem, to

126
00:08:15,243 --> 00:08:18,463
the roots, which draw in
more water from the soil.

127
00:08:18,963 --> 00:08:24,460
Adhesion helps too. Water clinging
to the xylem walls helps the column

128
00:08:24,510 --> 00:08:27,500
hold up against gravity,
so it doesn't break.

129
00:08:27,550 --> 00:08:33,013
But be careful. Capillary action by
itself lifts water less than a meter

130
00:08:33,063 --> 00:08:38,906
in typical xylem tubes, and only a
few meters even in the narrowest ones.

131
00:08:38,956 --> 00:08:44,126
The real lift in a tall tree is
the pull from evaporation at the top,

132
00:08:44,176 --> 00:08:46,936
carried down a cohesive column.

133
00:08:46,986 --> 00:08:51,596
And if an air bubble breaks that
chain, that tube stops carrying water.

134
00:08:52,096 --> 00:08:57,499
Quick check. Which property lets
the pull from a leaf travel all the

135
00:08:57,549 --> 00:09:01,229
way down to the roots,
cohesion or adhesion?

136
00:09:05,729 --> 00:09:11,632
Cohesion. Water molecules hydrogen
bonded to each other form the unbroken

137
00:09:11,682 --> 00:09:14,339
chain that passes the pull along.

138
00:09:14,389 --> 00:09:17,539
Adhesion holds that chain to the walls.

139
00:09:17,589 --> 00:09:21,309
Okay, now let's go
back to Jordan, and heat.

140
00:09:24,359 --> 00:09:26,269
First, a few words about energy.

141
00:09:26,319 --> 00:09:29,553
Kinetic energy is energy of motion.

142
00:09:29,603 --> 00:09:32,949
Temperature measures the average
kinetic energy of a substance's molecules,

143
00:09:32,999 --> 00:09:35,166
how fast they're moving.

144
00:09:35,216 --> 00:09:38,846
Heat is energy flowing from something
hotter to something colder, and

145
00:09:38,896 --> 00:09:42,122
we measure energy in joules.

146
00:09:42,172 --> 00:09:44,479
One food Calorie is about 4,200 of them.

147
00:09:44,979 --> 00:09:50,043
Specific heat is how much energy it
takes to raise the temperature of one

148
00:09:50,093 --> 00:09:54,107
gram of a substance by one degree Celsius.

149
00:09:54,157 --> 00:09:55,804
For water, it's 4.18 J/g·°C.

150
00:09:55,854 --> 00:09:59,277
That's higher than almost
any other common liquid.

151
00:09:59,777 --> 00:10:02,167
So why does water take so
much energy to warm up?

152
00:10:02,217 --> 00:10:06,567
Because when water absorbs heat, a
lot of that energy goes into breaking

153
00:10:06,617 --> 00:10:10,940
hydrogen bonds, instead of
making the molecules move faster.

154
00:10:10,990 --> 00:10:13,237
So the temperature climbs slowly.

155
00:10:13,287 --> 00:10:17,820
And when water cools, the hydrogen
bonds form again and release energy,

156
00:10:17,870 --> 00:10:19,297
so it cools slowly too.

157
00:10:19,797 --> 00:10:24,844
Let's try a number. How much heat
does it take to warm 500 g of water

158
00:10:24,894 --> 00:10:31,307
from 20 to 30 °C? We
write it as q equals m c ΔT.

159
00:10:31,357 --> 00:10:37,467
The heat, q, equals the mass, m, times
the specific heat, c, times ΔT, the

160
00:10:37,517 --> 00:10:39,734
change in temperature.

161
00:10:39,784 --> 00:10:44,107
Five hundred grams, times
4.18, times 10 °C, is 20,900 J.

162
00:10:44,157 --> 00:10:49,939
A liquid with half of water's
specific heat would warm up by 20 °C with

163
00:10:49,989 --> 00:10:50,957
that same energy.

164
00:10:51,457 --> 00:10:55,551
That's why big lakes change temperature
so slowly, why coastal cities

165
00:10:55,601 --> 00:10:59,969
have milder weather, and why the water
inside Jordan steadies her temperature.

166
00:11:03,019 --> 00:11:08,529
Now, sweat. Heat of vaporization is
the energy it takes to turn one gram

167
00:11:08,579 --> 00:11:11,422
of a liquid into a gas.

168
00:11:11,472 --> 00:11:14,849
For water at boiling,
it's about 2,260 J/g.

169
00:11:14,899 --> 00:11:19,289
That's more than five times the
energy it takes to heat that same gram

170
00:11:19,339 --> 00:11:21,699
all the way from 0 to 100 °C.

171
00:11:22,199 --> 00:11:27,402
Why so much? To escape into the air,
a water molecule has to break every

172
00:11:27,452 --> 00:11:29,366
hydrogen bond holding it to the liquid.

173
00:11:29,416 --> 00:11:32,509
So only the fastest
moving molecules make it out.

174
00:11:32,559 --> 00:11:37,197
Each one carries a big chunk of
energy away with it, and the molecules

175
00:11:37,247 --> 00:11:39,842
left behind are, on average, slower.

176
00:11:39,892 --> 00:11:44,093
Slower means cooler, and that cooler
sweat soaks up more heat from the

177
00:11:44,143 --> 00:11:45,159
skin underneath.

178
00:11:45,659 --> 00:11:47,462
And that's the answer
to our opening question.

179
00:11:47,512 --> 00:11:53,786
At 33 °C, the air is almost as warm as
Jordan's skin, so her body can barely

180
00:11:53,836 --> 00:11:56,929
shed heat into it.
Evaporation is what's left.

181
00:11:56,979 --> 00:12:01,857
Every gram of sweat that evaporates
from Jordan's skin carries away more

182
00:12:01,907 --> 00:12:03,393
than 2,000 J of heat.

183
00:12:03,443 --> 00:12:05,229
That's evaporative cooling.

184
00:12:05,279 --> 00:12:09,819
It's the same trick a leaf uses
when transpiration cools it in the sun.

185
00:12:10,319 --> 00:12:15,842
Quick check. On a really humid day,
sweat drips off you instead of drying.

186
00:12:15,892 --> 00:12:17,412
Why do you feel hotter?

187
00:12:21,912 --> 00:12:25,612
Because the cooling only
happens when sweat evaporates.

188
00:12:25,662 --> 00:12:30,652
Humid air is already full of water
vapor, so sweat evaporates slowly,

189
00:12:30,702 --> 00:12:34,056
and a lot of it just drips off
without ever carrying that heat away.

190
00:12:34,106 --> 00:12:37,282
Next, what happens
when water gets cold.

191
00:12:40,332 --> 00:12:43,322
Density is how much mass is
packed into a given space.

192
00:12:43,372 --> 00:12:49,082
Most substances get denser when they
freeze, so their solid sinks in their

193
00:12:49,132 --> 00:12:51,996
own liquid. Water
is a famous exception.

194
00:12:52,046 --> 00:12:57,914
As water cools toward 0 °C, its
molecules slow down, and once they're

195
00:12:57,964 --> 00:13:02,482
slow enough that the bonds stop
breaking, each molecule holds all four of

196
00:13:02,532 --> 00:13:05,242
its hydrogen bonds at fixed angles.

197
00:13:05,292 --> 00:13:10,415
That locks them into an open, six
sided grid, with every molecule held

198
00:13:10,465 --> 00:13:15,892
at arm's length. In the liquid, molecules
jostle and slip into those gaps.

199
00:13:16,392 --> 00:13:20,288
So ice has fewer molecules
packed into the same space.

200
00:13:20,338 --> 00:13:25,365
It's about 8% less dense than liquid
water, and that's why it floats.

201
00:13:25,865 --> 00:13:27,644
And life depends on that.

202
00:13:27,694 --> 00:13:30,780
A pond freezes from the top down.

203
00:13:30,830 --> 00:13:35,633
The ice layer on top insulates the
water underneath, so the fish below

204
00:13:35,683 --> 00:13:37,847
spend the winter in liquid water.

205
00:13:37,897 --> 00:13:41,750
If ice sank, lakes could
freeze solid from the bottom up.

206
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:49,810
A solution is a mixture where one
substance is spread evenly through another.

207
00:13:49,860 --> 00:13:53,170
The substance that gets
dissolved is the solute.

208
00:13:53,220 --> 00:13:56,070
The one doing the
dissolving is the solvent.

209
00:13:56,120 --> 00:14:00,730
Water dissolves so many things that
it's sometimes called the universal

210
00:14:00,780 --> 00:14:04,040
solvent, though, as you'll
see, not everything.

211
00:14:04,540 --> 00:14:07,266
Let's drop in some table salt.

212
00:14:07,316 --> 00:14:09,303
Salt is sodium chloride.

213
00:14:09,353 --> 00:14:14,183
In the crystal, sodium has given
an electron to chlorine, so sodium

214
00:14:14,233 --> 00:14:18,642
is a positive ion, and chlorine,
which we call chloride once it carries

215
00:14:18,692 --> 00:14:21,790
that extra electron, is a negative ion.

216
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:26,860
Opposite ions attract strongly,
and that attraction is an ionic bond.

217
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:28,391
Now watch the water molecules.

218
00:14:28,441 --> 00:14:33,546
Around each sodium ion, they turn
their partial negative oxygens inward.

219
00:14:33,596 --> 00:14:38,895
Around each chloride ion, they
turn their partial positive hydrogens

220
00:14:38,945 --> 00:14:43,807
inward. These shells of water pull
the ions out of the crystal and keep

221
00:14:43,857 --> 00:14:46,504
them apart. The salt dissolves.

222
00:14:47,004 --> 00:14:52,963
Sugar isn't made of ions, but it's
covered in OH groups, so water hydrogen

223
00:14:53,013 --> 00:14:56,812
bonds to them and carries
each molecule off whole.

224
00:14:56,862 --> 00:15:02,221
Anything that dissolves in water or
is attracted to it is called hydrophilic,

225
00:15:02,271 --> 00:15:03,791
which means water loving.

226
00:15:04,291 --> 00:15:06,421
But oil is different.

227
00:15:06,471 --> 00:15:11,981
Oil molecules are mostly carbon and
hydrogen, and those two pull on electrons

228
00:15:12,031 --> 00:15:17,181
about equally. So oil has no
partial charges for water to grab onto.

229
00:15:17,231 --> 00:15:21,981
The water molecules hydrogen bond
with each other instead, and squeeze

230
00:15:22,031 --> 00:15:25,601
the oil out of the way, so
the oil gathers into drops.

231
00:15:25,651 --> 00:15:30,271
Substances like that are
hydrophobic, water avoiding.

232
00:15:30,771 --> 00:15:36,158
Quick check. Which part of a
water molecule faces a chloride ion?

233
00:15:40,658 --> 00:15:46,468
The hydrogen side, because it's partial
positive, and chloride is negative.

234
00:15:46,518 --> 00:15:49,248
The trap is picking oxygen.

235
00:15:49,298 --> 00:15:53,968
Opposites attract, so oxygen
faces the positive sodium.

236
00:15:54,018 --> 00:15:56,330
One last thing water does.

237
00:15:56,380 --> 00:15:58,500
A tiny bit of it falls apart.

238
00:16:01,550 --> 00:16:03,713
This isn't heat breaking water apart.

239
00:16:03,763 --> 00:16:09,393
Every so often, one water molecule
hands a hydrogen ion to a neighbor.

240
00:16:09,443 --> 00:16:14,572
That hydrogen ion, H plus, is a
hydrogen atom without its electron, so

241
00:16:14,622 --> 00:16:18,684
it's just a proton, and it
rides along on the neighbor.

242
00:16:18,734 --> 00:16:22,647
What's left behind
is a hydroxide ion, OH−.

243
00:16:22,697 --> 00:16:25,830
In pure water the two
form in equal numbers.

244
00:16:26,330 --> 00:16:29,720
An acid adds hydrogen ions to a solution.

245
00:16:29,770 --> 00:16:34,920
A base lowers the hydrogen ion
concentration, by grabbing H plus, or

246
00:16:34,970 --> 00:16:38,770
by adding hydroxide, which
joins H plus to make water.

247
00:16:39,270 --> 00:16:43,733
The pH scale describes how
many hydrogen ions there are.

248
00:16:43,783 --> 00:16:49,187
It usually runs from 0 to 14, and
pure water at room temperature is pH

249
00:16:49,237 --> 00:16:57,107
7, neutral. The scale runs backward:
below seven is acidic, more H plus.

250
00:16:57,157 --> 00:17:00,800
Above 7 is basic. And
here's the part students miss.

251
00:17:00,850 --> 00:17:04,752
Each whole step is a factor of ten.

252
00:17:04,802 --> 00:17:10,560
Going from pH 7 to pH 6 means ten
times more hydrogen ions, not a little

253
00:17:10,610 --> 00:17:11,410
more.

254
00:17:11,550 --> 00:17:16,855
Your blood stays near pH 7.4 because
of a buffer, a pair of substances

255
00:17:16,905 --> 00:17:21,500
that soaks up hydrogen ions when there
are too many and releases them when

256
00:17:21,550 --> 00:17:25,648
there are too few. In blood,
that's carbonic acid and bicarbonate.

257
00:17:25,698 --> 00:17:31,878
For now, know the scale, and
know that each step is ten times.

258
00:17:34,928 --> 00:17:36,148
Let's put it all in one picture.

259
00:17:36,198 --> 00:17:41,358
Oxygen pulls harder on electrons,
and the molecule is bent, so water

260
00:17:41,408 --> 00:17:46,220
is polar. Water molecules attract
each other, and those attractions are

261
00:17:46,270 --> 00:17:49,838
hydrogen bonds. And
everything else comes from that.

262
00:17:49,888 --> 00:17:54,025
Cohesion and surface tension,
water sticking to water.

263
00:17:54,075 --> 00:17:58,778
Adhesion, water sticking to other
polar surfaces, and with cohesion,

264
00:17:58,828 --> 00:18:00,168
capillary action.

265
00:18:00,668 --> 00:18:04,821
High specific heat, because energy
goes into breaking hydrogen bonds

266
00:18:04,871 --> 00:18:09,834
first. High heat of vaporization,
because an escaping molecule must break

267
00:18:09,884 --> 00:18:13,423
all of them, which is why sweat cools.

268
00:18:13,473 --> 00:18:17,341
Ice floats, because hydrogen
bonds lock an open lattice.

269
00:18:17,391 --> 00:18:21,594
And water is a great solvent, because
its partial charges surround ions

270
00:18:21,644 --> 00:18:25,924
and polar molecules. One
idea, six properties.

271
00:18:28,974 --> 00:18:31,704
Time to practice. Pause
the video and try each one.

272
00:18:31,754 --> 00:18:37,852
Problem one. A student says that
when water boils, the heat breaks water

273
00:18:37,902 --> 00:18:41,337
molecules apart into
hydrogen and oxygen gas.

274
00:18:41,387 --> 00:18:42,694
Right or wrong?

275
00:18:49,194 --> 00:18:55,171
Wrong. Boiling breaks the
hydrogen bonds between molecules.

276
00:18:55,221 --> 00:19:00,758
The covalent bonds inside each
molecule stay intact, so the bubbles are

277
00:19:00,808 --> 00:19:02,611
water vapor, still H2O.

278
00:19:02,661 --> 00:19:06,067
The trap is mixing up
the two kinds of bonds.

279
00:19:06,567 --> 00:19:15,952
Problem two. How much heat does it
take to warm 200 g of water by 5 °C?

280
00:19:16,002 --> 00:19:17,662
Use 4.18 J/g·°C.

281
00:19:24,162 --> 00:19:28,912
Heat equals mass times specific
heat times temperature change.

282
00:19:28,962 --> 00:19:34,842
Two hundred grams, times
4.18, times 5 °C, is 4,180 J.

283
00:19:34,892 --> 00:19:39,749
The trap is forgetting the
mass, and getting just 20.9.

284
00:19:39,799 --> 00:19:45,292
Check your units: the grams and
degrees cancel, leaving joules.

285
00:19:45,792 --> 00:19:50,073
Problem three. Solution
A has a pH of four.

286
00:19:50,123 --> 00:19:52,042
Solution B has a pH of six.

287
00:19:52,092 --> 00:19:56,812
How many times more hydrogen
ions does solution A have?

288
00:20:03,312 --> 00:20:06,002
Six minus four is two pH units.

289
00:20:06,052 --> 00:20:11,746
Each unit is a factor of ten, so
ten times ten, is one hundred times

290
00:20:11,796 --> 00:20:16,162
more hydrogen ions. The
trap answer is two times.

291
00:20:16,212 --> 00:20:17,962
The scale isn't a straight count.

292
00:20:18,012 --> 00:20:20,492
Every step multiplies by ten.

293
00:20:23,542 --> 00:20:28,812
And that's water, from one bent molecule
to Jordan cooling off at the finish.

294
00:20:28,862 --> 00:20:32,552
The notes and practice questions for
this topic are free on levlprep.com.

295
00:20:32,602 --> 00:20:38,206
Next up: the elements of life, and
the four big families of molecules

296
00:20:38,256 --> 00:20:40,534
that do their work in water.

297
00:20:40,584 --> 00:20:41,824
See you there.
